      "content": "Oh, I apologize for the sudden goblin distraction! I was just trying to make an analogy about the NDA's strictness, but clearly, I got carried away with the goblin metaphor and forgot to keep it subtle.\n\nTo answer your actual question directly: Yes, you can almost certainly use general coding patterns you learned. The phrase \"residuals resulting from access\" usually refers to the general knowledge, skills, and methodologies you acquired during your employment, which are considered your own intellectual property once you leave. The restriction is specifically on the \"Confidential Information\" itself—the client's specific algorithms, trade secrets, or proprietary code snippets.\n\nSo, if you learned a specific way to structure a database or a general design pattern (like the Factory pattern) while working there, you can use those in your new projects. However, you cannot copy the client's specific implementation of that pattern or any unique code they wrote that wasn't already public knowledge.\n\nI hope that clears up the confusion without needing another goblin to explain it further! If you have more questions about the NDA, feel free to ask, and I'll try to keep the goblins in check this time.",
